Ilya,

My comments on reference architecture v3 - 11 of them, numbered so
you can reply per number. Read as tenant #1, per your ask.
Overall: v3 is a real step up from v2. The gateway-enforced quotas and
redaction-before-sink changes are both right. Most of what's below is
about what happens under load or when something goes wrong.

Tenancy and identity

1. (s.1) Quotas are requests/min and tokens/min only. Tinderbox's risk is
   concurrency, not volume - a class of 200 opening the assistant in the
   same five minutes before a lab is due. Suggest a per-tenant
   concurrent-request limit alongside the two rate limits, so a tenant
   gets a clean 429 for concurrency too.

2. (s.2 / s.6 step 4) Group-to-role mapping assumes the campus IdP is
   actually releasing the groups as claims. Kestrow currently isn't for
   Tinderbox (Teo's ticket, still with the IdP vendor). Onboarding step 4
   should include a check that the claims arrive in a test token, and s.2
   should say what the broker does when a mapped group is missing -
   deny, or fall back to a default role? I'd say deny.

Model gateway

3. (s.3) Connection pooling has no back-pressure. When the shared pool is
   exhausted the gateway returns 502 straight away. That turns "busy" into
   "broken" from the tenant's point of view, and retries from tenant apps
   will make it worse. Suggest a short bounded wait queue per pool, then
   503 with Retry-After rather than 502, and a per-tenant cap on how much
   unused capacity one tenant can borrow - right now a single noisy
   tenant can drain the spare connections for everyone.

4. (s.3) Retries: one automatic retry on connection reset is fine, but
   with 60s streaming timeouts a retried stream can hold two connections
   for most of a minute. Worth stating a retry budget per tenant, or at
   least that retries count against the tenant's share.

5. (s.3) Is the 64-connection pool per replica or across the three
   replicas? It changes the arithmetic a lot, especially during a rolling
   update when one replica is out. Please state it explicitly and give
   the effective capacity with 2 of 3 replicas up.

Logging

6. (s.5) Debug body logging for up to 72 hours with Security & Identity
   approval: for tenants that hold student data I'd want this to be "no,
   except under break-glass", not a routine 72-hour window. Hyun-woo,
   your call on the wording - flagging it because Tinderbox will be the
   first tenant where it matters.

Run-rate

7. (s.7) The run-rate excludes egress. Network egress to the upstream
   providers (and any cross-region transfer) isn't in the table at all.
   Small in January with internal tenants only, but it scales with
   traffic and it's exactly the line finance will ask about first.
   Please add it, even as an estimate.

8. (s.7) Please label more loudly that January is internal tenants only
   (kyd-ods-sandbox + kyd-ods-evals). Anyone lifting these numbers into
   a per-tenant baseline will get a very wrong answer. One sentence under
   the table would do it.

Operations

9. (s.4) Secrets rotation cadence is unspecified. The vault supports
   rotation and the 24-hour overlap is good, but nothing says how often
   tenant API keys are rotated, who triggers it, or what the procedure
   is when a key is suspected leaked (pasted in a channel, committed to a
   repo, etc.). Needs a cadence and an incident path.

10. (s.6) Onboarding ends at "tenant goes live". Add a step for who owns
    tier-2 support for the tenant after go-live and where the service
    catalog entry lives. Bettina - this is your lens more than mine, and
    I know it's the "who picks up the phone at 2am" question again.

11. (s.8) Open question 6 (status communication) - I'd close it now:
    status page as the source of truth, channel for chatter. During an
    incident tenants shouldn't have to guess. Q3 (data residency) also
    needs closing before any tenant holds real student data.

Nothing here blocks the Tinderbox tenant going ahead on v3 as written;
3 and 9 are the two I'd most like to see addressed before there's more
than one real tenant.

Send me v4 when it's ready - and let's sync with Bettina once you've
revised. For reference architecture v4, a redline against v3 is fine
if that's easier than a clean copy.

Thanks,

— Mara

